# Design

## Reading of the brief

This is a browser-only, human-vs-human variant of recognizable tic-tac-toe:
the board is 3×3, X and O alternate, and the normal three-in-a-row win
condition remains intact. The brief allows the variant rules to be invented, so
the ambiguity around avoiding draws is resolved with a ninth-move tiebreaker.

The implementation is static HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. `game.js` contains
state transitions and validation without DOM access. `ui.js` only creates and
updates DOM elements and delegates moves to `game.js`. `proof.js` imports the
same rule module and exhaustively walks every legal move sequence.

## Rules considered

The chosen rule is: a full board with no line is won by the player who made
the final move. This preserves normal play and requires only one clear
additional rule.

An alternative was to remove marks or change the board after a fixed number of
turns. That would also avoid draws, but would make the familiar placement
version harder to learn and prove. A random tiebreaker was rejected because it
would make the outcome depend on chance rather than the players' moves.

## Why every game has a winner and terminates

After every move, `game.js` checks all eight possible winning lines. Therefore,
any line-making move ends the game with that player as winner. If no line is
made, each move fills one previously empty square. There can be at most nine
such moves. On the ninth move the board is full, and the ninth mover is
declared the winner even if no line exists. Thus no terminal state is a draw,
and no line of play can continue past nine moves.

Run `node proof.js` to exhaustively enumerate every legal game tree from the
empty board. The proof checks that every leaf is terminal, every terminal leaf
has a winner, and that the maximum observed game length is nine moves.

## Known limitations

There is no computer opponent, persistent score, backend, or network
dependency. Those are outside the requested scope. The UI is intended for
current Chrome.
